Here is the feedback we got for generated code for model_training.py in the conversation:

<FEEDBACK>- Balance the latent blocks before kNN: z_prior (n_sets ≫ k_free) dominates distances. Standardize each latent dimension to zero mean/unit variance on train, or explicitly rescale blocks (e.g., scale z_prior by sqrt(k_free/n_sets)) before building neighbors. This alone can markedly improve cluster separability and thus ARI.
- Try multiple representations for clustering and pick by unsupervised criteria: run Leiden on z_prior only, z_free only, z_concat (after standardization) and select the best using a combination of graph modularity and stability; keep constraints on cluster count. This is label-free but better aligns with ARI.
- Follow the plan’s resolution policy: incorporate modularity into selection (currently stability-only). Also add bootstrap resampling of cells (not just random_state changes) for stability estimates. Persist chosen k, resolution, and modularity.
- Tune neighborhood graph construction for high-D latent: grid-search k ∈ {15, 30, 50} and metric ∈ {cosine, euclidean-after-whitening}; pick via modularity+stability. Optionally use mutual kNN/Jaccard pruning to sharpen communities.
- Fix batch mixing during training to make MMD effective and improve cluster consistency: use a balanced batch sampler ensuring each minibatch has ≥2 cells per dataset; switch to multi-kernel MMD (mixture of bandwidths). If needed, add a lightweight adversarial batch classifier on z_free with gradient reversal to directly reduce dataset predictability.
- Prevent overfitting via dataset-specific decoder biases that can leak batch effects into latents: add an L2 penalty on bias_dataset and constrain per-gene dataset offsets to zero-mean across datasets each step; monitor their variance.
- Make the sparsity prior biologically sharper and scale-robust: replace mean|z_prior| per-set with a group-lasso style penalty (L2 across batch per set, then L1 across sets), and normalize by sqrt(set_size) so large sets aren’t over-penalized. Decouple λ_s defaults from the global scale to avoid double-scaling; tune global scale on val modularity.
- Encourage informative latents without collapse: add KL free-bits per latent (e.g., min-KL of 0.5–1.0 nats per dim) or a cyclical KL schedule; this often yields better-defined clusters and higher ARI than a monotonic ramp to a fixed β.
- Improve reconstruction weighting for clustering-relevant signal: upweight HVGs (or 1/dispersion) in the NB loss so informative genes drive the embedding; keep weights fixed across splits for consistency.
- Use log library size as an encoder covariate (in addition to decoder offset) to reduce residual size effects in z; this improves neighborhood purity.
- Add cross-block decorrelation explicitly: penalize only the cross-covariance between z_prior and z_free (rather than full Cov(z)−I), preserving within-block structure that benefits clustering.
- Post-training ensembling to smooth the embedding: average latents from the best few checkpoints (SWA) or enable test-time dropout and average multiple encodings; recluster the averaged embedding and select via modularity+stability.
- Consider batch-aware graph construction at inference (no labels used): optionally build neighbors with BBKNN/Harmony on the latent using dataset as batch key, then run Leiden; in practice this improves integration and ARI while remaining unsupervised w.r.t. cell types.
- Robustness in clustering backend: evaluate Louvain alongside Leiden and keep the better unsupervised score; HDBSCAN as a fallback for datasets with variable density.
- Log and monitor what correlates with ARI: per-epoch neighbor modularity on a fixed latent snapshot, mean and sparsity of |z_prior|, block norms (||z_prior|| vs ||z_free||), batch entropy in local neighborhoods. Use these to trigger early-stopping/checkpoint selection closer to clustering quality than ELBO alone.</FEEDBACK>

