<LM_OUTPUT> {"role":"biology","feedback":"Clustering approach\n- Build an SNN/Jaccard-weighted graph or mutual-kNN to reduce chaining on cosine kNN and improve Leiden stability. Example: use kneighbors_graph to get binary kNN, compute shared-nearest-neighbor weights (Jaccard), and pass as igraph weights.\n- Log the chosen resolution, modularity, and bootstrap stability to a small JSON (e.g., cluster_metrics.json add keys resolution, modularity, stability) for traceability.\n- Consider adding a seed-sweep consensus (e.g., 5 seeds per resolution) to de-noise resolution selection; choose resolution by highest average stability across seeds subject to 8–40 clusters.\n\nMetric computation\n- ARI on test looks correct. Also record test set size (n_test_used) to ensure ARI is computed on a substantial subset and to catch accidental filtering.\n\nDEG quality\n- rank_genes_groups on log1p CPM is fine, but add minimal detectability and effect filters to avoid lncRNA/antisense dominance: set rankby_abs=True, and post-filter by pct_in >= 0.1, pct_out <= 0.1, and log2FC >= 0.5 before top-10 selection. Include pct_in, pct_out, and log2FC in the summary for interpretability.\n- Increase minimum cluster size for DE testing from 5 to at least 20 cells to reduce small-sample artifacts; skip DE for smaller clusters.\n- Ensure DE is run on the exact gene universe used for modeling: restrict to var['used_in_model'] and, if available, protein-coding only (e.g., var['gene_biotype']=='protein_coding'). This will eliminate many non-informative lncRNAs seen in current top markers.\n- If scanpy Wilcoxon fails and you hit the fallback, do not compute fold-changes on log-transformed data. Convert back with np.expm1 first, then compute mean-based log2 fold-change on linear scale.\n- Prefer a replicate-aware test to guard against residual batch effects: do pseudobulk per dataset (or protocol) within each cluster and use a Wilcoxon/t-test on pseudobulk replicates; or, if available, use scVI differential expression with dataset as covariate.\n\nPathway/marker summaries\n- Use the planned latent prior signals directly: compute per-cluster mean z_prior and report the top gene sets by magnitude. This is more faithful to the prior-guided model than DEG-based overlap and will mitigate off-target GO terms observed (e.g., neural/urogenital terms in PBMC).\n- For enrichment, align the universe to the DE gene universe (the same var subset used for DE). Currently you use all genes in adata_all, which biases p-values. Build the background from intersect(used_in_model, prior-mapped genes).\n- Apply multiple-testing correction across all tested sets per cluster (e.g., BH FDR) and report q-values; rank by q-value then overlap.\n- Incorporate the provided prior_gene_symbol_map.csv in enrichment to resolve synonyms/aliases beyond simple uppercasing; map both DEGs and adata var to the standardized prior symbols via this map.\n- Filter out overly broad sets during enrichment (e.g., keep 10 ≤ set_size ≤ 500) to avoid generic GO terms dominating the top results.\n\nBiological coherence checks\n- Add a quick QC asserting that each major immune lineage (T, B, NK, Mono/DC) has at least one cluster enriched for the appropriate marker sets; if not, flag in logs to revisit resolution or DEG filters.\n\nImplementation snippets\n- SNN weights: A = kneighbors_graph(emb, n_neighbors=30, mode='connectivity'); W = (A @ A.T).multiply(1.0 / (A.sum(1) + A.sum(1).T - (A @ A.T))).A for Jaccard; build igraph with W upper-triangle > 0.\n- DE post-filter: df = sc.get.rank_genes_groups_df(adata, group=g); df = df[(df.pvals_adj < 0.05) & (df.pct_in >= 0.1) & (df.pct_out <= 0.1) & (np.abs(df.logfoldchanges) >= 0.5)].\n- Enrichment FDR: for each cluster, run BH on all tested sets’ p-values, add qval field, and keep top 10 by qval.\n- z_prior summary: if obsm['z_prior'] exists, compute per-cluster mean and take top 10 sets by absolute mean; include names from prior metadata in cluster_summary.json."} </