<FEEDBACK>- Build a sharper neighbor graph to reduce chaining and overconnection that blur true cell types:
  - Use mutual-kNN or SNN (Jaccard) weights instead of plain cosine kNN; prune edges below a similarity/Jaccard threshold.
  - Prefer UMAP connectivities (local scaling) as edge weights; they typically yield higher-ARI Leiden partitions than raw cosine.
  - L2-normalize rows and whiten latent dimensions (unit variance per dim) before neighbor search to prevent high-variance axes from dominating cosine.

- Exploit the biology-aligned latent to better reflect true cell types:
  - Cluster on z_prior only or down-weight z_free (e.g., scale z_free by <1) before kNN; this usually increases ARI by emphasizing marker/pathway structure.
  - If both are used, re-scale each block (z_prior, z_free) to comparable norms to avoid imbalance.

- Tune graph hyperparameters jointly with resolution:
  - Grid-search k over {10, 15, 20, 30, 40, 50} and resolution over a finer grid (e.g., 0.1 steps); pick the pair with maximal unsupervised stability (bootstrap ARI) and high modularity within the 8–40 cluster constraint.
  - Add consensus across seeds/resamples (e.g., 5 seeds) and use consensus clusters for assignments; this consistently bumps ARI by de-noising stochastic variability.

- Hierarchical refinement instead of one-shot Leiden:
  - First find coarse clusters at low resolution to capture major lineages; within each large cluster, re-run Leiden at cluster-specific resolutions chosen by local stability/silhouette. This reduces both over- and under-clustering and typically improves ARI.

- Post-processing to fix borderline cells and over-splitting:
  - Identify high-confidence “core” cells per cluster (e.g., cells whose kNN majority is their own cluster across multiple bootstraps) and reassign ambiguous cells by kNN majority vote to cores.
  - Merge cluster pairs with high inter-cluster connectivity and low between-centroid distance (and minimal DE signal) to prevent artificial splits; enforce a minimum cluster size (e.g., ≥30) by merging smaller ones to nearest neighbors.

- Split-aware robustness aimed at test ARI (without labels):
  - Compute stability separately within the test split subgraph and optimize a combined score (e.g., harmonic mean of global and test-only bootstrap stability) when picking k/resolution.
  - Balance graph construction across splits/datasets (equal subsampling per dataset during bootstrap or weighting edges to avoid dominance by train/val), improving generalization and test ARI.

- If permissible to use labels on non-test data, add a semi-supervised model selection step:
  - For each k/resolution candidate, compute ARI on the validation split only (using obs['cell_type']) and pick the best; this usually transfers to higher test ARI while avoiding test leakage.</FEEDBACK>

