<LM_OUTPUT> {"role":"data_science","feedback":"Key fixes to align preprocessing with the spec and improve robustness:\n- Don’t drop non-prior genes before HVG. Compute HVGs on the full standardized gene set, then set used_in_model = (HVGs ∪ prior-covered genes) \\ {MT,RPS/RPL}. For prior matrices, pad M with zero rows and expand L with zero rows/cols for HVG-only genes so indexing matches the final gene order.\n- Enforce the exact MT filter: remove cells with percent.mt > 20 (not a group-dependent threshold). Keep the robust count/feature and 1%/99% trims as-is.\n- Compute HVGs with raw counts to match seurat_v3 assumptions: sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', n_top_genes=3000, batch_key='dataset', layer='counts', inplace=True). This avoids double-logging and aligns with per-batch union behavior.\n- Tighten detection filtering for prior genes: avoid keeping completely unobserved genes. Replace keep_det = keep_det | gene_in_prior with keep_det = keep_det | (gene_in_prior & (detected > 0)) so only genes observed in at least one cell survive pre-HVG; zero-train safeguards can remain as a second line.\n- Prevent information leakage: perform the stratified split first, then compute HVGs and detection thresholds on train only (with batch_key restricted to the train subset), finally union with prior genes and propagate the resulting masks to val/test by gene index.\n- Gene cap fairness: when guaranteeing ≥ MIN_PER_SET_COVERAGE per set, iterate sets in a round-robin or priority-queued manner instead of single pass over set indices to avoid early sets monopolizing the budget; then fill remaining slots by the current global rank (prior_set_counts, nbatches, mean_expr, HVG flag).\n- Minor consistency: keep layers['counts'] as raw int32 CSR (already done), X as log1p CPM float32 (done), persist var['used_in_model'] and mapping arrays for both prior order (prior_index) and model-gene order (done)."} </LM_OUTPUT>

