Here is the feedback we got for generated code for data_preprocess.py in the conversation:

<FEEDBACK>- Compute HVGs before prior alignment and on the full deduplicated gene space. Then take used_in_model = (HVGs ∪ prior-covered genes) \ {MT/RPS/RPL} and only afterward realign M/L to the filtered gene order. Aligning to prior first discards non-prior HVGs and weakens clustering signal.

- Fix detection filtering for prior genes. You currently OR keep_det with gene_in_prior, which retains even never-detected prior genes. Require minimal detection for prior-covered genes (e.g., ≥1 cell or PRIOR_GENE_MIN_DET_FRAC) to avoid injecting dead features that destabilize NB training and dilute HVG signal.

- Enforce the percent.mt cutoff per spec. Don’t raise the threshold above 20% via MAD/quantiles; apply absolute percent.mt > 20 removal per group to prevent high-MT outliers from leaking into training.

- Refine model-gene capping. The hard MIN_PER_SET_COVERAGE across all sets forces low-quality genes into the model when sets are numerous. Instead, prioritize by: HVG flag, set-degree, per-gene mean expression, and set information content; optionally guarantee coverage only for top-ranked sets (by metadata weights/IC). This preserves pathway coverage without degrading the feature pool.

- Avoid duplicating library size in covariates. You already pass libsize for NB; dropping log_library_size from obsm['covariates'] (or standardizing all covariates and recording mean/std) reduces depth leakage into FiLM and improves batch invariance.

- Strengthen stratified splits. Use domain||protocol||dataset as the stratification key (not just dataset||protocol) to preserve domain balance, and enforce at least 1 val and 1 test per group when n≥3. Persist the split RNG seed in metadata (already present) and validate per-group proportions.

- Preserve extra nuisance covariates. If obs has donor, sample, chemistry/kit, or lane, retain and one-hot them (with deterministic category orders) so downstream can optionally condition (FiLM) or penalize (MMD) on richer batch structure.

- Standardize encoder inputs on train only. Compute per-gene mean/std over train counts-normalized log1p CPM, store in var and uns, and optionally write a z-scored layer for encoder inputs. This stabilizes optimization and improves latent separation.

- Make covariates consistent across splits. You already persist category orders; also store the exact one-hot column list and per-column statistics (mean/std) and verify them upon reload to prevent drift across environments.

- Broaden ribosomal exclusion. Tag and exclude MRPS*/MRPL* (mitochondrial ribosomal) and optionally HBA*/HBB* hemoglobin genes from modeling to reduce known technical biases.

- Keep sparse operations sparse. In the per-set coverage pass and prior-activity embedding, avoid per-set dense conversions; precompute sparse boolean membership once and index vectorized to reduce memory spikes and speed up large runs.

- Expose diagnostic masks. Persist var flags for HVG, prior_covered, detection_kept, and final used_in_model, and save per-set post-filter sizes. This enables targeted ablations and faster iteration when tuning selection.

- Normalize and record prior matrices. Column-normalize membership (and sym-normalize L) before saving, and record normalization in metadata so decoder init/regularization can use consistent scales.

- Avoid preprocessing-stage clustering artifacts collision. Either remove the preprocessing KMeans/ARI outputs or namespace them distinctly (e.g., preprocess_clustering_*) to prevent downstream confusion and metric mix-ups.

- Guard against batch dominance. If any dataset constitutes >90% of train, optionally downsample or record sample weights for training to improve batch-invariant learning and ARI.</FEEDBACK>

