Here is the feedback we got for generated code for data_preprocess.py in the conversation:

<FEEDBACK>- Fix the gene capping rank order. np.lexsort uses the last key as primary; your current (-mean_expr, -degree, -nbatches, -rank_hvg) makes HVG the primary sort, not expression or prior coverage as intended. Reorder keys so genes with strong prior coverage (ideally weighted by membership strength) and HVG status sort first, then tie-break by mean expression and nbatches. This directly biases selection toward informative, pathway-aligned features, improving clustering signal.
- Use weighted prior coverage, not just degree. Since prior_gene_set_membership encodes weights (IDF-like), rank genes by the sum (or max) of their membership weights instead of simple set count. This favors specific markers over generic GO-heavy genes and should boost biological separability.
- Tighten and simplify the detection filter. Apply the same 0.5% train-only detection threshold to both prior and non-prior genes: keep_det = (frac >= 0.005) & (detected > 0). Admitting ultra-rare prior-only genes (0.1%) injects noise and dilutes cluster boundaries.
- Apply the <0.5% detection drop before HVG selection. Removing near-constant genes prior to HVG avoids spurious variability estimates and yields a cleaner HVG pool, especially helpful in single-dataset scenarios.
- Limit nuisance covariates. Restrict obsm['covariates'] to one-hots for domain, protocol, and dataset only. Extra encodings (donor/sample/chemistry/kit/lane) invite over-conditioning and can wash out biological structure critical for unsupervised clustering.
- Realign gene order to the prior and drop unmatched genes before building M/L. Working in the exact prior order (no padding rows) reduces sparse-zeros in M/L, tightens Laplacian regularization to relevant genes, and makes masked-decoder initialization cleaner and more effective.
- Improve set selection pressure toward informative pathways. In the top-set round-robin, prioritize by information content (e.g., type/source priority, higher per-set weights, and penalize very large sets). Guarantee coverage for high-priority, compact marker sets first; avoid biasing toward the largest GO terms.
- Persist train-only per-gene mean counts for decoder init. Store a vector of mean counts (or CPM) per model gene for use in W_prior initialization (weights × mean expression). Better init reduces early training drift and yields clearer z_prior structure for clustering.
- Remove the dense X_zscore layer. It densifies the full matrix and adds memory overhead without being used downstream. Persist only train_mean/train_std; let training do on-the-fly standardization if needed. This frees memory to increase batch size or seed broader hyperparameter sweeps downstream, indirectly improving ARI.
- Make synonym map parsing schema-aware. When loading prior_gene_symbol_map.csv, prefer explicit columns (original_symbol, standardized_symbol, status) and skip mappings with status ≠ “mapped.” This prevents mis-mapped or deprecated symbols from polluting the alignment.
- Don’t over-prune biology by default. Excluding hemoglobin (HBA/HBB) as “technical” is not in spec and may remove genuine biological signal in some datasets. Keep MT and ribosomal exclusions as specified; gate hemoglobin behind a dataset-specific switch if needed.</FEEDBACK>

