This conversation is potentially part of a larger system. The output is used as model evaluation

Here is the feedback we got for generated code for model_training.py in the conversation:

<FEEDBACK>- Use train-only z-scoring for encoder inputs. Pull mean/std from preprocess_metadata.encoder_preferences.input_stats (var.train_mean_log1p_cpm and var.train_std_log1p_cpm) and apply to X across all splits. Relying on raw X (or a non-existent X_zscore) inflates scale variance across genes, hurting both training stability and downstream clustering distances.

- Consume the filtered set weights produced by preprocessing. Load artifacts.set_weights_filtered_json from preprocess_metadata and map by set_id to lambda_s. Do not rescale again by 1/sqrt(set_size); that double-normalizes and weakens the intended per-set sparsity signal.

- Make the MMD batch-invariance penalty actually bind:
  - Compute MMD across dataset_idx (db), not the ultra-fine group_idx (gb). With group_idx, most batches have <2 samples per group so mmd ≈ 0 for the entire run.
  - Balance the DataLoader by dataset (not by group), and ensure ≥2 samples per dataset per batch. At minimum, reshuffle group_keys each epoch and cap groups per batch, but the robust fix is to construct BalancedBatchSampler over dataset_idx.

- Reduce free-branch dominance (logits_free_frac ~0.98–0.99 throughout):
  - Add column L2 normalization for W_free at end of each epoch (mirror W_prior’s projection) and add a small column-norm penalty for W_free (same scale as prior_colnorm_penalty).
  - Increase orthogonality_penalty (e.g., 5e-4) and/or W_free weight_decay (e.g., 5e-3) to disincentivize the free branch from explaining everything.
  - Temporarily freeze W_free for the first 20–30 epochs (then unfreeze) so the model must engage the prior branch during warm-up.
  - Either cut k_free to 16 or add a modest L2 penalty on z_free magnitude (mean(z_free^2) with 1e-4–3e-4) and/or apply a higher KL weight for z_free than for z_prior (branch-specific KL multipliers).

- Feed numeric covariates to the encoder FiLM. Concatenate standardized log_library_size (from obsm['covariates_numeric'] in preprocessing) to the one-hot domain/protocol/dataset when building Cov*, so cov_dim includes this numeric effect.

- Slightly strengthen prior structure without overfitting:
  - If W_prior columns remain noisy after fixing free dominance, increase laplacian_alpha (e.g., to 3e-3) and/or prior_nonneg_penalty an order of magnitude to promote smooth, additive pathway loadings.
  - Keep the end-epoch W_prior column re-normalization; do not remove it.

- Improve clustering robustness in high-dimensional latent space:
  - Reduce dimensionality of Z representations before kNN using train-fit PCA/whitening (e.g., 64 dims) to mitigate curse-of-dimensionality with 800+ dims.
  - Expand the grid: k ∈ {15, 30, 45}; alpha_prior_weight ∈ {0.5, 0.8, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0}; resolutions ∈ {0.4, 0.8, 1.2, 1.6}. Also always evaluate z_free-only and z_prior-only paths explicitly; pick the best by the current unsupervised criterion.
  - Continue z-scoring reps by train split before neighbor building; keep tiny-cluster merging but allow the merge if it improves modularity or silhouette.

- Sampler hygiene and determinism:
  - Randomize the iteration order of groups each epoch in BalancedBatchSampler to avoid systematic truncation when you slice oversized batches down to batch_size.
  - Log the per-batch unique dataset count and per-dataset batch sizes to verify MMD viability; alert if any dataset has <2 samples per batch.

- Validation/early stopping objective alignment:
  - If you increase/decrease any regularizer (MMD, orthogonality, W_free penalties), include those terms in the val_total exactly as in train so early stopping tracks the true objective.

- Monitoring targets to verify fixes:
  - Aim for logits_free_frac ≈ 0.6–0.85 by mid/late training, nonzero train/val MMD, stable mean_abs_zprior (no collapse), and improved test-split silhouette. If free still dominates, further tighten W_free regularization or lower k_free.</FEEDBACK>

