Here is the feedback we got for generated code for downstream_analysis.py in the conversation:

<FEEDBACK>- Broaden and prioritize the graph search to reduce batch-driven edges and escape local optima:
  - Evaluate both UMAP connectivities and SNN-Jaccard graphs (you already implement both) and select the better one per (k, res) by the same test-weighted silhouette + modularity criterion. Also try a mutual-kNN (MKNN) variant (keep only reciprocal neighbors) to cut spurious cross-batch edges.
  - Add a small alpha grid to blend graphs (W = α W_umap + (1−α) W_snn, α∈{0.25,0.5,0.75}); prune weak edges via {0, “p10”, 0.01}. Cache all W to keep runtime bounded.
  - Expand k to {15, 30, round(sqrt(n)), 50} and res to a slightly wider adaptive grid (e.g., 0.2–1.4 in 0.2 steps). For each (k, res), run Leiden with multiple seeds (e.g., 3–5) and keep the best seed by the selection score to avoid unlucky partitions.

- Use the train-anchored graph you already implemented (_anchor_graph_on_train) as an alternative candidate:
  - Score both full and train-anchored graphs and choose the one with higher test-weighted silhouette after a light batch penalty. This prevents test-only communities and typically boosts test ARI when splits are heterogeneous.

- Actually implement the z_prior vs z_free scaling you report in metrics:
  - Read n_sets and k_free from EMBEDDING_META_PATH (or infer via a first-pass correlation structure) and try alpha scales for the first n_sets dims (e.g., {0.5, 1, 2, 4}) before graph building. Select alpha jointly with (k, res) by the same score. This often sharpens lineage separation and improves ARI.

- Make whitening a first-class, train-only option in the grid (whiten=True/False) and select it by the same score. You already have the hooks; toggling it in the candidate loop can reduce batch-driven variance and improve ARI.

- Add a lightweight stability term only for shortlisted candidates to keep runtime low:
  - After ranking candidates by silhouette+modularity, compute bootstrap_stability (n_boot≈5, sample_frac≈0.7) for top 3–5 candidates and add it as a tie-breaker. This avoids the previous runtime hit while rescuing unstable high-silhouette partitions.

- Strengthen post-hoc refinement to fix both over- and under-fragmentation:
  - After refine_assignments, apply your _attempt_split_large_cluster to clusters with low test-silhouette; accept splits only if both test-silhouette and modularity improve.
  - Conversely, merge near-duplicate clusters when their mean z_prior profiles are highly correlated (e.g., Pearson > 0.95) and their top DEGs substantially overlap (>70% of top-50). Accept the merge only if test-silhouette does not drop. This reduces label fragmentation that depresses ARI.

- Replace the AMI-based batch penalty with a normalized conditional entropy H(batch|cluster)/log(|batch|), weighted by cluster size (higher is better). This is more stable when batches are imbalanced and avoids over-penalizing benign alignments that can hurt ARI.

- Enforce size-aware small-cluster handling: set min_cluster_size adaptively in refine_assignments (e.g., max(15, ceil(0.005·n))) and penalize candidate solutions with excessive micro-clusters (e.g., >10% cells in clusters < min_cluster_size). This curbs unstable tiny groups that reduce ARI.

- Add a final test-only reassignment pass for ambiguous cells:
  - For cells with low core-neighbor agreement on test, reassign using weighted votes restricted to train-core neighbors (if available); otherwise, fall back to centroid in the normalized space. This reduces test-specific drift without using labels.

- Use consensus across seeds/graphs to stabilize assignments:
  - Build a co-association matrix from the top 3 candidate partitions and run a single Leiden pass on it to derive a consensus labeling; accept if test-silhouette improves. This often boosts ARI by smoothing seed/graph idiosyncrasies.

- Tighten candidate acceptance gates to favor biologically coherent solutions without labels:
  - Require a minimum fraction of clusters to show coherent pathway structure: e.g., at least 70% of clusters must have ≥3 strongly biased z_prior sets (|mean activity| above a dataset-specific MAD threshold). Use this as a soft penalty in selection; empirically, such partitions align better with true cell types and raise ARI.

- Housekeeping/metrics:
  - Only emit warning_low_stability if stability was actually computed; otherwise omit the flag. Also log test coverage explicitly alongside ARI, and add NMI as a secondary readout (kept out of selection).</FEEDBACK>

