<FEEDBACK>- Reduce free-branch dominance to improve clusterability and prior interpretability:
  - Stage unfreezing: keep W_free frozen longer than the prior branch and unfreeze it gradually; give W_free a much lower LR (or its own optimizer group with 10–20× smaller LR) plus stronger regularization (higher wfree_colnorm_penalty, z_free_l2, orthogonality). Zero-initialize W_free so it contributes nothing during warmup.
  - Capacity/pressure: shrink k_free (e.g., 16) and increase kl_free_mult; add a direct penalty that targets the free-branch dominance you already monitor (e.g., quadratic penalty on logits_free_frac above a target like 0.3–0.5) so optimization explicitly suppresses overuse of the free residual.
  - Gate contribution: introduce a time-dependent coefficient c_t on logits_free that ramps 0→1 after the prior branch has stabilized.

- Make the prior decoder truly masked and nonnegative:
  - Parameterize W_prior as softplus(Wp_raw) and multiply by the binary mask in forward; register a gradient hook to zero gradients outside the mask. This enforces exact zeros and nonnegativity without relying on tiny penalties and prevents wasted updates to masked positions.
  - Normalize masked columns only on unmasked entries (project back onto the mask after normalization) to avoid drift.

- Strengthen batch invariance where it actually matters:
  - Compute MMD over a finer batch key (e.g., dataset||protocol) and use that same grouping for the sampler. The current sampler often yields batches with a single dataset; redesign it to always include multiple distinct groups per batch (sample K distinct groups per batch and draw evenly from each). Log datasets_per_batch_mean using that finer key and target ≥2 consistently.
  - Make MMD more robust by averaging multiple RBF kernels with different bandwidths (e.g., median, 0.5×median, 2×median) rather than a single gamma.

- Prevent covariate leakage that harms invariance:
  - Restrict FiLM covariates in the encoder to domain/protocol (and numeric libsize); exclude dataset one-hots from FiLM and keep dataset effects in the decoder bias only. This reduces encoding of batch into z.

- Improve KL scheduling and stability:
  - Use branch-specific KL schedules (prior KL reaches target earlier; free KL ramps slower/longer). Consider a short cyclical KL schedule to avoid early posterior collapse and improve separability.
  - Normalize λ_s so the average group-L1 magnitude is stable across different n_sets (e.g., scale λ_s to unit mean), then ramp its global scale after the prior branch starts training.

- Keep the likelihood simple and stable:
  - Default to NB unless zero-inflation is extreme; raise the ZINB switch threshold (global zero fraction >0.8) or defer ZINB to a later fine-tuning phase to avoid overparameterization that can blur clusters.

- Tighten optimization controls for W_free:
  - Separate optimizer group with much smaller LR and higher weight decay; optionally add cosine-with-restarts for W_free only so it catches up without spiking.
  - Clip W_free gradients more aggressively than the rest (per-parameter max-norm) to prevent the post-unfreeze spikes seen in the logs.

- Clustering selection sensitivity:
  - Don’t hard-cap PCA to 64; pick n_components to explain ≥90–95% variance (upper-bounded), which often preserves more separable axes in high-K latent spaces.
  - Try both cosine and euclidean on whitened space; some datasets separate better under euclidean after whitening.

- Monitoring/early stopping hygiene:
  - Smooth the validation objective with an EMA before applying patience to avoid plateau jitter around the best epoch; continue logging logits_free_frac and datasets_per_batch_mean after the sampler/MMD changes to verify the intended dynamics.</FEEDBACK>

